## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend known 64-bit bit tricks to 128-bit values without branches. Emphasizes portability and optimization.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parallel bit counting
- Masks and shifts across 64-bit lanes
- Combining results from hi/lo halves

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0x7F);
}
unsigned countBits128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you use hardware intrinsics if allowed?
- How would you extend to 256-bit types or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses proficiency in low-level bit manipulation using only shifts and masks—skills essential for systems programming, embedded development, and cryptographic code. It demonstrates understanding of parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR/SIMD Within A Register) techniques.

## Key Concepts
- **Bit masking and shifting:** Extracting and repositioning specific bit ranges
- **Parallel processing:** Operating on multiple data elements (bytes) simultaneously via mask patterns
- **Byte-local vs. global reversal:** This approach reverses bits within each byte independently, unlike global bit-reversal algorithms
- **Magic constants:** The hexadecimal masks (`0xF0F0F0F0`, `0xCCCCCCCC`, `0xAAAAAAAA`) are standard patterns for these swaps

## Complete Code Example
```c
uint32 reverseBitsInEachByte32(uint32 x) {
  // Step 1: Swap adjacent nibbles (0xF0 = 11110000 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xF0F0F0F0) >> 4) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);

  // Step 2: Swap adjacent 2-bit pairs (0xCC = 11001100 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xCCCCCCCC) >> 2) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);

  // Step 3: Swap adjacent individual bits (0xAA = 10101010 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xAAAAAAAA) >> 1) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);

  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
