questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
This question tests precise bit-manipulation skills across 128-bit data without loops or branches. It validates the ability to split work across halves and combine results safely while demonstrating understanding of efficient parallel counting algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining two 64-bit counts to produce a 0-128 result
- Bitmask-based parallel computation

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x3FULL;
}

uint64_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  return popcount64(hi) + popcount64(lo);
}
```

## Examples
- `popcount128(0x8000000000000000ULL, 0x0000000000000001ULL)` returns 2 (bits at positions 127 and 0)
- `popcount128(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL)` returns 128 (all bits set)

The SWAR algorithm processes all 64 bits in parallel using bitmasks to accumulate counts efficiently.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive complex bit-pattern metrics with constant-time bit tricks, not brute force.

## Key Concepts

- Identify run starts with n & ~(n << 1)
- Implement a portable popcount64 using parallel masks
- Edge-case handling for 0 and large runs

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

static inline unsigned countRuns64(uint64_t n) {
  return popcount64(n & ~(n << 1));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to an 128-bit value?
- What are the performance implications on large-scale bitmap indexes in practice?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of a classic bit-twiddling pattern to find the next permutation of a fixed-popcount bitset in constant time, without loops or branches. It also probes edge cases like overflow and zero input.

## Key Concepts

- Snoob algorithm for next higher with same popcount
- 64-bit overflow behavior and edge cases (n == 0 or max with k ones)
- Pure bitwise/shifts approach, no loops

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t nextWithSamePopcount(uint64_t n) {
  uint64_t smallest = n & -n;
  uint64_t ripple = n + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = n ^ ripple;
  return ripple | ((ones >> 2) / smallest);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary word size or signed integers?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures?
